text:
mina: Noticing a parity gap between the Browser SDK and the Node/Server SDK in the new Next.js agent starter. The example uses a streaming generator and relies on being able to cancel the stream when the client disconnects, but in the server SDK the stream iterator doesn't expose a cancel/abort hook, so serverless invocations sometimes hang until timeout.

ryan: Oof. Which starter? The agent-demo in examples/next-agent-stream?

mina: Yep. Flow is: client opens EventSource to /api/agent-stream, server starts SDK.stream({prompt}) and iterates sending SSE chunks. If the client closes the connection early (navigate away), the iterator keeps running. In Vercel serverless this accumulates and we hit concurrency issues.

zoe: Do we get any logs that show the handler still running after the response is closed?

mina: We do. Handler doesn't exit until the SDK stream completes or the lambda times out. No abort signal is propagated.

ryan: Two quick mitigation ideas: (1) hook into the HTTP response 'close' event and signal the SDK (if it supports AbortSignal). (2) As a sample-app fallback, wrap the iterator with a timeout/early-cancel that stops after N seconds in dev to avoid resource leaks.

dev-rel: The Browser SDK has a .cancel() helper that callers use when user navigates away; if the server SDK had an option to accept an AbortSignal or expose a .return() on the async iterator we could wire it cleanly. Could be a SDK parity issue.

samir: Agreed. I dug into the server SDK code: streaming is implemented as an async generator but the exported object doesn't accept a signal param. Adding support for AbortSignal to the stream method should be straightforward: check signal.aborted and throw on abort.

mina: Small repro snippet from the example (simplified):
```js
// pages/api/agent-stream.js
export default async function handler(req,res){
  res.setHeader('Content-Type','text/event-stream');
  const it = sdk.stream({prompt: req.body.prompt});
  for await (const chunk of it){
    res.write(`data: ${JSON.stringify(chunk)}\n\n`);
    if (res.writableEnded) break; // but this doesn't catch everything
  }
  res.end();
}
```

zoe: The `res.writableEnded` check is fragile. On some platforms the stream remains writable but the client disconnected. Node exposes `res.connection`/`req.socket` close events. But a better approach is an AbortSignal passed into sdk.stream and wiring req.on('close', () => controller.abort()).

ryan: Can we implement a thin server-side helper in the example until SDK parity lands? Something like:
```js
const ac = new AbortController();
req.on('close', () => ac.abort());
for await (const chunk of sdk.stream({prompt, signal: ac.signal})) { ... }
```

samir: Yeah that's the ideal helper. I'll open a small PR to the example that adds this pattern plus a comment explaining why it's necessary. Also add a test that simulates req close by calling emitter.emit('close') and asserts iterator stops.

dev-rel: On the SDK side I filed a tracking ticket to support AbortSignal on stream and to ensure async iterator return() calls propagate to underlying model runtime to free KV cache resources. We should also document best practices in the devex guide: "How to stream safely in server environments".

mina: Two additional notes for the PR: (A) Keep the helper opt-in behind a comment so users on edge runtimes see guidance for streaming there. (B) Add a small troubleshooting entry in the sample README: "If functions hang when clients disconnect, use AbortController pattern or avoid long-running model sessions in serverless."

ci-bot: Heads-up: examples CI will run stream tests; make mocks deterministic (don't rely on real model streams). Mark the new test as fast/mock-only to avoid flaky timing.

ryan: Also add a short snippet showing how to do the same with the App Router / server actions pattern so folks migrating don't copy the old pages/api pattern incorrectly.

zoe: Thanks — that will cut down on support tickets where teams deploy the starter and see unexpected lambda exhaustion.

samir: PR opened: 2755554350-stream-abort-helper-and-test. It includes the AbortController example, a mock-based unit test that verifies iterator.return is called, and a README troubleshooting blurb. Can someone review?

dev-rel: Reviewing now. Minor ask: ensure the example uses `signal` param name documented in the SDK ticket, and add a short note about edge vs node runtimes.

mina: Thanks all. Once SDK adds signal parity we can update the example to remove the workaround and link to the SDK changelog. :thumbsup:
